// Multivariate normal random vectors (MASS's mvrnorm), and the symmetric square root of a covariance matrix that
// mediation's mediate draws its parameters with. Written for rStats.js.
//
// Sources: the specification oracle/mediation-behaviour.md, from MASS's documentation and published output
// (experiments/mediation116.R, mediation116-eigensign.R; every attempt in experiments/mediation116-log.md); the 2 x 2
// eigendecomposition is a port of LAPACK 3.12.0's SRC/dlaev2.f (modified BSD; see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).
// - mvrnorm(n, mu, Sigma) = mu + V diag(sqrt(max(lambda, 0))) Z' with Sigma = V diag(lambda) V' (eigenvalues
//   decreasing) and Z an n x p matrix of standard normals filled by column (p blocks of n draws)
// - Its values depend on the signs of the eigenvectors. R takes them from LAPACK's dsyevr: for 2 x 2 matrices that is
//   dlaev2's closed form (ported here, so the values are R's); for 3 or more dimensions dsyevr's MRRR path (dstemr)
//   picks the signs from intermediate quantities, which is not ported (the user's decision 2026-11-25): the vectors
//   come from rStats.js's eigen, the draws have R's distribution but not R's values
// - The symmetric square root V diag(sqrt(max(lambda, 0))) V' does not depend on the signs. It is computed with the
//   cyclic Jacobi method on the matrix itself, which finds the eigenvalues of a graded matrix (variances on very
//   different scales, such as the covariance of coefficients of variables x 1e150 and x 1) to relative accuracy; LAPACK's
//   (and EISPACK's) methods lose the small ones, so R's draws break down there (experiments/mediation116-edge.R). At
//   ordinary scales the result equals R's to rounding
// - 3 or more dimensions in mvrnorm: the same Jacobi decomposition, each eigenvector's largest component positive
